# One name per concept

Pick one name for each concept and use it everywhere in that project: prose, tables, figure captions, code, configs, and READMEs. Do not rotate synonyms for variety.

```markdown
<!-- Bad -->
users, then customers, then members, then people
contacts, friends, connections, network
profile page, CV, member page
job ad, job post, listing, opening

<!-- Good -->
members (the people who joined)
connections (the members a member has linked to)
profile (what a member shows about themselves)
job listings (what an employer posts)
```

Lock a short glossary when a piece of work starts. Use the locked word in running text, headings, figures, and code. Do not switch to a synonym later for style.

Example, using the vocabulary of a professional network such as LinkedIn:

| Concept | Use | Do not mix in |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A person who joined | member | user, customer (except in billing code) |
| A member linked to another member | connection | contact, friend |
| What a member shows about themselves | profile | page, CV |
| A job an employer posts | job listing | ad, post |

If a source already named a field (the `Headline` field on the profile form), keep that proper name. Do not invent a parallel nickname beside it.
